## Contents
## 1. Introduction
With the development of economy and society, environmental pollution has become more and more serious, a large amount of toxic and hazardous waste has been produced [1–3]. These toxic substances are eventually discharged into the water and result in the sewage formation, causing serious health problems [4–6]. Therefore, removal of toxic waste from wastewater is imminent. The composition of sewage mainly includes heavy metals and organic pollutants. People have used the bioelectrochemical action of microorganisms to effectively treat organic pollutants in sewage [7,8]. However, heavy metals in inorganic pollutants (heavy metals refer to metals with a density greater than $4.5~\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ ) cannot be biodegraded. The toxic heavy metals in industrial wastewater treatment include mercury $\left(\mathrm{Hg}\right)$ , cadmium (Cd), chromium (Cr), lead (Pb) and arsenic (As). Heavy metals interact strongly with proteins and enzymes in the body, making them inactive, and may also accumulate in certain organs of the body, causing chronic poisoning [3]. Therefore, it is very necessary to remove toxic heavy metals from sewage.
Mercury has a wide range of applications, such as glass mercury thermometers, batteries, fluorescent lamps, pigments, pesticides, and chemical catalysts. However, the improper treatment results in serious mercury pollution. Mercury is a heavy metal that can be accumulated in organisms. By monitoring surface water, scientists have found that mercury pollution is the main heavy metal pollution in China, and Huang et al. evaluated the biological toxicity of the above five heavy metals in the order of ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{g}}^{2+}>{\mathrm{Pb}}^{2+}>{\mathrm{Cr}}^{6+}$ $>{\mathsf{A}}s^{5+}>{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{d}}^{2+}$ . The results show that mercury ions are the most toxic, and a small concentration can cause the death of the bug [9]. The Minamata disease incident in Japan has caused widespread panic and paid a terrible price. The chief culprit is mercury chloride discharged from chemical plants into seawater. Mercury ions are particularly stable when combined with sulfur ions. The structure of proteins in the human body has many sulfur-containing groups, and mercury can easily combine with thioredoxinreductase in the brain, deactivating the protein and causing brain death [10,11]. The reason why mercury ions are a great threat to human health is that inorganic mercury ions are easily biochemically formed into methylmercury, and the toxicity is increased several times. Unlike arsenic ions, arsenic ions are methylated to form low-toxic dimethyl arsenic. And because of the presence of methyl in methylmercury, it improves its fat solubility, making it extremely easy to be absorbed by the body, the absorption rate can reach $100\%$ [12]. The two accidental drops of methylmercury on the hand caused the tragedy of scientist Karen Wetterhahn. Seriously, any form of mercury in the environment can be transformed into methylmercury with sharply increased toxicity under certain conditions. Therefore, it is particularly important to remove mercury ions before converting to methyl mercury. The World Health Organization (WHO) has set the highest level of mercury pollution in drinking water at 1 ppb, the low emission standards for mercury ions also increase the difficulty and challenge of removal.
In order to remove mercury ions in sewage, many methods including chemical precipitation method [13], liquid extraction method [14], ion exchange method [15], electrolysis method, metal reduction method and so on, have been used. Because of its low cost and simple operation, the adsorption method [16,17] becomes the best and most popular method at present. The adsorption method is mainly through the interaction between adsorbent and adsorbate (such as electrostatic attraction, van der Waals force, chemical bond) to achieve the purpose of adsorbing adsorbate. Adsorption methods can be roughly divided into three categories: physical adsorption, chemical adsorption, and ion exchange adsorption. In the actual adsorption process, these three types of adsorption actually occur together. For the adsorption method, the choice of adsorbent is particularly important.
Because of the strong coordination of mercury ions with sulfur and nitrogen, sulfur-rich metal sulphide [18,19], metallothionein [20], and thiol-functionalized mesoporous silicon [21], mesoporous carbon [22], biomagnetic materials [23], Clay [24]; sulfhydryl, amino-functionalized carbon nanotubes [25]; and nitrogen and sulfur-containing hydroxides [26,27] etc. have been used in the study of adsorption of mercury ions. In addition, sulfur-rich and nitrogen-rich polymers [28–32] are also used in the adsorption of mercury ions Sulfur and nitrogen-functionalized materials become a major trend in mercury materials. So people have designed materials from the adsorption capacity rather than the mechanism. Therefore, the frame material with a porous structure and a large specific surface area-- metal organic framework MOF has become a people’s preferred choice.
The MOF material is a framework material formed by coordination of metal ions or metal clusters with organic ligands. Due to the characteristics of the MOF material itself, such as large specific surface area, designable frame structure, controllable pore size, metal unsaturation sites, easy to modify etc. [33–35], MOFs has been widely used in gas separation [36–38], gas storage, photocatalysis [39,40], drug loading [41,42], sensing [43,44] and other fields. Certainly, the adsorption such as gas adsorption [45,46], liquid adsorption [47,48], heavy metal adsorption [49] are also the primary function of MOF applications. MOF materials have attracted more and more attention because of their high efficiency, simplicity and energy saving ability to remove mercury ions. In view of the great advantage of MOF powder material in adsorption, people also try to broaden the diversity of materials. As the combination of MOF material and membrane material, MOF membrane material has attracted extensive attention. On the basis of the advantages of MOF powder materials, the characteristics of membrane materials (flexibility, etc.) are introduced. And, compared to MOF powder, MOF membranes are easier to recycle and more suitable for largescale applications. It makes MOF materials more competitive in wastewater treatment. This review aims to provide more researchers with a more intuitive and clear understanding of the MOF and MOF membrane on mercury metal ion removal.
## 2. The removal of mercury ions based on MOF powder materials
The effect of the excellent adsorption properties of pure MOF materials were firstly explored by Abd El Salam’s group. They used microwave synthesized Mn-MOF for removing mercury ions. Thanks to the advantages of the MOF material itself (pores), the mercury removal efficiency of Mn-MOF is $65.19\%$ [50]. From this result, the pure MOF materials have a certain adsorption effect of mercury removal, but need to be improved. Therefore, people try to improve the adsorption of heavy metals through simple modification of MOF materials. Xiong et al. formed MOF-74 that can efficiently remove trace mercury ions by introducing hydroxyl groups on the ligand [51]. Similarly, Yang et al. synthesized ZrMSA by introducing ligands containing sulfur side chains, which showed high adsorption capacity and ultra-fast adsorption kinetics, removing $99.99\%$ of mercury ions in $5\mathrm{min}$ [52]. This shows that proper functionalization or modification of MOF materials can effectively improve the adsorption of mercury ions. The significant improvement of the adsorption effect is due to the combined effect of the characteristics of the MOF material (large specific surface area and excellent pore structure) and the coordination effect of the modified sulfur ligand on the mercury ion. Inspired by this result, we tried to compare and summarize by consulting a large amount of literature, and finally reviewed the influence of different modification types and modification methods on the adsorption performance of mercury ions. We mainly talk about the mercury removal performance of functionalized MOF materials, including sulfur-functionalized MOF, nitrogen-functionalized MOF, nitrogen-sulfur co-functionalized MOF, MOF composites and MOFs-derived materials.
## 2.1. Sulfur ligand-based MOF powder materials
Based on the strong coordination between mercury ions and sulfur, the removal of mercury ions can be achieved by sulfurizing the material. The sulfurized ligands can be divided into thiolated ligands and thioetherated ligands (as shown in Fig. 1). Therefore, this part is divided into two aspects: thiolated MOF and thioetherified MOF for the removal of mercury ions.
## 2.1.1. Thiolated MOF powder materials
The sulfurized MOF may be a sulfurization process of the ligand before synthesizing the MOF material, thereby obtaining a pre-sulfurized MOF material. Pre-sulfurization treatment is mostly the sulfurization of the ligands, which can introduce sulfurcontaining groups into MOF without changing the topological structure of MOF. This also broadens the application of homogeneous MOF materials.
Yee et al. introduced sulfhydryl groups at the 2, 5 position of the classic ligand (1, 4-terephthalic acid), formed $Z\mathrm{r}$ -DMBD MOF (as shown in Fig. 2a) that can efficiently adsorb mercury ions. Based on the soft and hard acid-base theory [53], the MOF formed by the coordination of high-valent metal ions has high stability, because sulfur is close to the $Z\mathrm{r}{-}0$ cluster and Zr-DMBD has good water stability, which is the premise and advantage of Zr-DMBD for adsorption of mercury ions. It is worth mentioning that the mercury removal efficiency of activated Zr-DMBD material exceeds $99.9\%$ and even reduces the concentration of mercury ion solution to below 0.01 ppm [54]. On this basis, Ding et al. conducted indepth studies on the conditions and mechanism of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -DMBD adsorption of mercury ions. The effect of $\mathsf{p H}$ on mercury ion adsorption is shown in Fig. 2b. The optimal adsorption environment was determined to be a slightly acidic $\mathsf{p H}6$ , under which the maximum adsorption capacity of mercury ions was $171.5{\mathrm{~mg/g}}$ . The inferred mechanism was also verified by experiments :
$$
\begin{array}{r}{-\mathrm{SH}+\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}+2\mathrm{NO}_{3}-...\mathrm{S}-\mathrm{S}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Hg}^{+}+\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{NO}_{3}-}\end{array}
$$
Mercury ions coordinate with sulfur by replacing hydrogen in sulfhydryl groups, and the substituted hydrogen enters the solution to form hydrogen ions that regulate the PH of the solution [55]. Leus et al. also used $Z\mathrm{r}$ -DMBD for mercury ion adsorption research, confirming that $Z\mathrm{r}$ -DMBD material has good recycling and regeneration performance [56]. The mercury removal performance of these materials is shown in Table 1. Table 1 summarizes in detail the mercury removal performance and characterization methods of sulfurized MOF materials.
With the increase of the sulfhydryl group density in the ligand, the adsorption capacity of mercury ions is enhanced. The introduction of polysulfhydryl group (see Fig. 1b, $\mathrm{H}_{4}{\mathsf{O M T P}}^{\prime}$ not only promoted the stability of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -OMTP, also promoted adsorption of mercury. Zr-OMTP can be stabilized in air for 18 days or even boiled in boiling water for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ After several mercury adsorption cycles, the structure remained highly ordered. The maximum mercury adsorption capacity is $403\ \mathrm{mg/g}$ and $10~\mathrm{mg}$ Zr-OMTP can reduce the mercury ion solution from 6.57 ppm to 1 ppb, with a very high removal efficiency [57].
Different from $Z\mathrm{r}$ -DMBD and Zr-OMTP, which introduce aryl mercaptan in the MOF framework, Zr-MSA (as shown in Fig. 3) firstly introduces alkyl mercaptan into the MOF material. Due to the higher mercury affinity of alkyl mercaptan and the high density of alkyl mercaptan in $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MSA, Zr-MSA can adsorb mercury ions up to $734~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , which shows significant adsorption capacity compared with the aforementioned MOF adsorbent. It is worth mentioning that the synthesis and activation process of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MSA uses environmentally friendly solvent water, effectively avoiding the use of toxic organic solvents [52].
The pre-functionalized MOF has been widely used in the adsorption and detection of mercury ions [66–69] due to its simple operation, but it still has some shortcomings. The modification of ligands may change some physical or chemical properties of ligands, such as solubility and stability. At this time, the synthetic conditions will also change accordingly. Post-synthetic modification is a simple modification on the basis of stable MOF, which avoids repeated exploration of complex synthetic conditions. In addition, post-synthetic modification can greatly regulate the type and number of functional groups introduced into MOF materials, which is beneficial to the controllable regulation of the performance of MOF. Next, we will elaborate the application of functionalized MOF after modification in the detection and adsorption of mercury ions.
Qiu et al. prepared mercapto modified MOF materials by using the coordination unsaturated metal center in HKUST-1 and the coordination bond with -SH group in dithioethanol (As shown in Fig. 4a). Among them, the metal unsaturated sites are formed by removing coordination solvent molecules by vacuum hightemperature treatment. The sulfhydryl functionalized HKUST-1 showed high adsorption affinity $\left(\mathrm{K_{d}}=4.73\times10^{5}\mathrm{mL/g}\right)$ and significant adsorption capacity $(714.29\mathrm{~mg/g)}$ for mercury ions. The excellent mercury removal ability is attributed to the large specific surface area and high density adsorption sites of MOF materials. The authors suggest that this establishes a simple and energy-saving route to achieve the removal of heavy metals by MOF-based adsorbents [62]. Similarly, Xu et al. synthesized SH@Cu-MOF in a similar way. The material can capture mercury not only in solution, but also in the vapor state. This expands the applicability of mercaptan modified MOF materials [63].
In addition, many scientists also use free functional groups on MOF ligands to form chemical reactions to realize the functionalization of MOF. For example, Li et al. introduce thioacetic acid into the channel walls of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-68 (In) by using the condensation reaction between amino and carboxyl groups to form SH-MIL-68 (In) (see Fig. 4b). SH-MIL-68 (In) has a rapid adsorption kinetics $(\mathrm{K}_{2}=1.25\mathrm{~g}\mathrm{\mg}^{-1}\mathrm{\min}^{-1})$ , showing that more than $98\%$ mercury ions can be removed within 2 min and that mercury ions in water can be reversibly adsorbed [64].
It is also possible to sulfurize the material through multiple post-modifications. Cr-MIL-101-AS (Fig. 4c) is one such example. After the two steps in series, the alkenyl and sulfhydryl groups were introduced into Cr-MIL-101 as adsorption sites for mercury ions [65]. Cr-MIL-101-AS has high adsorption capacity due to high density of adsorption sites. It is worth mentioning that this is the first example of sulfhydryl in MOF by means of covalent postmodification.
## 2.1.2. Thioetherized ligand-based MOF powder materials
The thiolated MOF material is widely used in water treatment because of its good adsorption effect. Due to the stronger force between the sulfhydryl group and the mercury ion, the material requires relatively complicated operations during desorption. This was avoided by introducing sulfides. while mercaptan requires the help of strong acids and bases, the sulfide group is more likely to reversibly adsorb and desorbe mercury ions. Stirring and extraction at room temperature can regenerate MOF materials [60].
Zhou et al. introduced ${\mathrm{-}}S C{\mathrm{H}}_{3}$ on 2, 3, 5 and 6 positions of terephthalic acid to form the ligand TMBD (Fig. 1b). PbTMBD [58] was synthesized by solvothermal method with lead nitrate. However, the existence of $\mathsf{P b}$ in MOF limits the practical application of materials, and the environmental impact of lead introduction must be considered. He et al. avoided potential environmental impacts by replacing lead nitrate with zinc nitrate which is more environmentally friendly. Also, a flexible long chain like - $-S C H_{2}C H_{2}C H_{3}S$ is introduced on the ligand (Fig. 1b, L) and has a stronger ability to combine mercury ions than -SH, - ${\cdot}S C\mathsf{H}_{3}$ and extends further into the channel region [59]. However, Zn-MOF and PbTMBD adsorb mercury ions in organic media and require high temperature heating.
For the sake of the ease of operation and practical application, it makes sense remove mercury ions in mild conditions and solvents. Thus, a series of water-stable MOF for removing mercury ions at room temperature was explored.
At room temperature, the saturation adsorption capacity of ZrM1 (Fig. 5a) to mercury ions in aqueous solution is $275~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . The good mercury removal ability of Zr-M1 is attributed to the abundant thioether groups on the ligand [60]. More importantly, the ${\bf Z}{\bf r}{\bf-M}{\bf O}{\sf F-H}{\bf g}{\bf C}{\bf l}_{2}$ formed after the adsorption of mercury ions greatly improves the order of the structure due to the insertion of mercury, so that the original disordered MOF materials, which are not suitable for single crystal $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray diffraction, can be treated and analyzed by SCXRD. Not only the purpose of removing mercury ions is achieved, but also the structure of the material can be analyzed.
Thioether of the ligand not only plays a role in adsorbing mercury ions, but also it is very effective for stabilizing the framework of MOF materials. He et al. designed and synthesized a series of ligand L1-L4 containing sulfide side chains (Fig. 1b) to explore the influence of sulfide side chains on framework stability and mercury adsorption. The modification of sulfur ether side chain can reduce the sensitivity of MOF to solvent loss. Taking the adsorption of mercury ions by Zr-L4 as an example, the introduction of sulfur side chain can simultaneously improve the binding affinity and framework stability of mercury ions. The removal efficiency of Zr-L4 for mercury ions is $96\%$ , and the distribution coefficient is $2.40\times10^{4}~\mathrm{mL/g}$ . Even Zr-L4 crystals soaked in a $\mathrm{PH}=1$ solution for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ remain highly crystalline [61]. In addition, the introduction of sulfur side chain can also improve the stability of
MOF material in air, which provides ideas for the application of gas–solid interface.
Zr-L4 can not only selectively adsorb mercury ions, but also detect mercury ions with electrochemical sensor. Fu et al. realized the selective adsorption and electrochemical sensing of mercury ions (Fig. 5b) with Zr-L4. The results show that the sulfur side chain plays an important role not only in the adsorption of mercury ions, but also in the electrochemical sensing of mercury ions. The sensitivity range of the prepared sensor is $0.01\ \mathrm{nM}$ to $3\mathrm{mM}$ the detection limit is $7.3~\mathrm{fM}$ and the sensor has good cyclic regeneration [70].
## 2.2. Ammoniated ligand-based MOF powder materials
Because mercury ions also interact strongly with nitrogen. Therefore, aminated MOF is also a good choice for adsorption of heavy metal ions. Table 2 summarizes the mercury removal performance of ammoniated MOF.
Similarly, Luo et al. synthesized a functionalized MOF (Fig. 6b) that can remove trace mercury ions in water using a hybrid ligand strategy. For mercury ion aqueous solution with ultra-low concentration, even the initial concentration of 2 ppb, the removal efficiency can reach $66.5\%$ , reducing the solution concentration to 0.68 ppb. Thanks to the rich amide groups and hydroxyl groups in the mixed ligands, $Z\mathrm{n}$ -hip has a good ability to adsorb mercury ions, with the theoretical maximum adsorption capacity up to $333~\mathrm{mg/g}$ [72]. Moreover, through thermodynamic analysis, the author found that the Gibbs free energy of mercury adsorption $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{w a s}}<0$ , which indicated that the adsorption of mercury ions by the adsorbent was spontaneous.
Post-synthetic modification is also a good strategy to avoid the above situation. Moreover, we can use post-modification methods to form more forms of ammoniated MOF, and further explore the adsorption of mercury ions by different ammoniated MOFs.
SALE - solvent assisted ligand exchange (see Fig. 7a) is a simple and efficient processing method in post-modification. Some ligands in the prepared MOF can be replaced by heterogeneous reaction with other ligand in a solvent. In general, the SALE strategy can avoid the destruction of the pore structure after MOF functionalization, and also avoid the influence on the synergistic effect of the functionalized ligand and pore structure. Ding et al. used water-stable 3D MOF as a precursor, synthesized a magnetic MOF that can adsorb and remove mercury ions through a single crystal to single crystal solvent assisted ligand exchange (SC SCSALE) strategy. When the original ligand is exchanged, the new ligand contains amino groups, the final synthesized MOF can be used to remove mercury ions [73]. In addition, the author also changed the structure and pores of Zn-MOF through SAST (solvent-assisted structural transformation) strategy (see Fig. 8a). The SAST strategy is similar to the SALE strategy in that it uses solvent assistance to change the structure of the MOF. However, there are differences. SALE exchanges new ligands into MOF, and SAST directly changes the structure on the basis of the original framework. For the sake of classification, the two are discussed together. The resulting structural symmetry is better and the porosity is higher. This change makes it (structurally modified Zn-MOF) more advantageous in the adsorption of heavy metal ions, and $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ in the ligand can be more distributed in the channels of MOF. The enrichment and removal of mercury ions can be achieved with high porosity and abundant amino [74]. Due to the improvement of porosity, even the encapsulation of organic dye molecules can be achieved. SAST can effectively re-edit and optimize the connection mode between metal ions and ligands, so as to adjust the structure’s porosity, and overcome the limitation of topological structure on porosity. Thus, the resulting structure has better spatial characteristics, broadening the application of materials.
MOF-808-EDTA (a trap based on MOF that can efficiently trap heavy metal ions) was also synthesized by solvent assisted ligand exchange. For mercury ions, a soft acid ion, MOF-808-EDTA obviously has a good removal effect and can reduce the solution concentration from ppm to ppb level. MOF-808-EDTA can efficiently capture 22 heavy metal ions including soft, hard and edge metal ions [76]. It has no selectivity and pertinence. Luo et al. proposed a case of MOF with high selectivity to mercury ions. They introduced Thymine into MOF through chemical reaction between amino and carboxyl groups to form MIL-101-thymine, which exhibits excellent selectivity (as shown in Fig. 8b) with a selectivity coefficient of 568.18. Because of the specific effect of mercury ion on thymine, mercury ion probes and sensors based on thymine have been extensively studied [75]. The authors extend this specific interaction to remove mercury ions from water.
Surprisingly, the ammoniated MOF can not only adsorb mercury ions, but also form a fluorescent probe to detect mercury ions. The author has indicated that Zr-DMBD had certain fluorescence quenching effect on mercury ions and mercury vapor [54], which provided some ideas for fluorescence sensing to detect mercury ions. Based on the luminescence mode of ligand–metal charge transfer (LMCT), many scientists use nitrogen on the ligand of MOF to react with mercury ions to block the ligand’s energy transfer to the metal (see Fig. 9), then showing a change in fluorescence [77–79]. Thus, the fluorescence sensor of functional MOF material was realized to detect mercury ions. Because of its advantages of high sensitivity and low detection limit, the MOF-based fluorescence sensor has been applied to detect mercury ions easily and efficiently.
Based on this effect, some dual-function materials have been developed for simultaneous detection and adsorption of mercury ions. Nin-NH-MIL-101(Al), a bifunctional adsorbent and detection sensor, by grafting ninhydrin onto MOF by covalent action between amino and ninhydrin. The material has long-term stability and can be stable for at least three months (stored away from light), and the sensing performance will not be affected. Nin-NH-MIL-101 (Al) can detect trace mercury ions, even at a concentration of 1 ppb, with excellent sensitivity (as shown in Fig. 10a). It can remove $92\%$ of mercury ions in the solution, with a saturation adsorption capacity of $127.4~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . And the adsorption of mercury ions is reversible (Fig. 10b). It has great potential not only for industrial wastewater treatment, but also for detecting and removing mercury ions from e-waste [80].
## 2.3. Nitrogen and sulfur co ligand-based MOF powder materials
Besides the single ligand, functionalized MOFs composed of the hybrid ligands can also be used to adsorb mercury ions (see Table 3). When MOFs is formed by mixed ligands, it is easier to form MOF materials with novel structure due to the change of coordination environment. The structure and performance of MOF can be directed by selecting appropriate ligands for specific applications [81].
FJI-H12 was synthesized in situ with the mixed ligand NCSgroup and Timt (2, 4, 6-Tri (1-imidazolyl) - 1, 3, 5-Triazine). A packed separation column loaded with FJI-H12 can continuously and rapidly remove mercury ions in aqueous solution. Because its large pore size can fully promote the diffusion of mercury ions and abundant SCN- adsorbs mercury ions, so that it has an excellent mercury ion adsorption capacity $(439.8\mathrm{mg/g)}$ , with the distribution coefficient of $1.85\times10^{6}\mathrm{mL/g}$ [82]. In addition, FJI-H12 can be synthesized in large quantities under very mild conditions, which makes large-scale applications possible. Similarly, a Nibased MOF was synthesized with hybrid ligand 3-bpd (1, 4-bis (3-pyridyl) -2, 3-diaza-1, 3-butadiene) and NCS-. The MOF had a mercury adsorption capacity of $713{\mathrm{~mg}}/{\mathrm{g}}$ , a removal efficiency of $94.3\%$ , and the color of the material changed from green to gray (Fig. 11) [83]. Although mercury ions can be easily detected by visualization, the adsorption process of mercury ions is irreversible. That is, the material cannot be recycled, which is one of its major defects. In 2016, Zhao et al. reported the first case of retrieving an MOF based sensor that could detect mercury ions [88]. The two carboxyl groups in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ (see Fig. 1b) in the mixed ligand are arranged along the line in a way that enables them to bridge by various coordination methods. This results in the porosity of the final structure, which is conducive to fluorescence sensing and pollutant adsorption removal. $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF realized reversible detection of mercury ion through iodide ion [89]. This provides some ideas for us to realize the recycling performance of dual-function materials that can simultaneously detect and adsorb mercury ions.
Rudd et al. synthesized a bifunctional LMOF-263 by introducing tppe (a chromophore-based ligand) (Fig. 12a) and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{d}{\sf b}\mathrm{tdc}0_{2}$ (Fig. 1b) that regulates the pore structure [84]. The sulfone group contained in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{d}{\sf b}\mathrm{td}\mathrm{c}0_{2}$ can react with mercury ions, Fig. 12b shows the mechanism of the interaction between $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{d}{\sf b}\mathrm{td}\mathrm{c}0_{2}$ and mercury. Also, LMOF-263 has pore structure and large specific surface area. LMOF-263 has a maximum mercury ion adsorption capacity of $380~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , which can remove $99.1\%$ of mercury ions in aqueous solution within $30\ \mathrm{{min}}$ . The emission intensity of LMOF-263 changed with the different concentration of mercury ion solution (as shown in Fig. 12c). And the MOF material is uniformly dispersed in the mercury ion solution, which can avoid the influence caused by the uneven dispersion of the material, and increases the advantage of the MOF material used as a fluorescent sensor for detecting mercury ion.
Similar to the sulfide MOF, the nitrogen-sulfur co-modified MOF can also be achieved through post-synthetic modifications. Through the chemical reaction between free amino groups in MOF and different other functional groups, a variety of MOFs capable of removing mercury ions can be formed. UiO-66-DMTD was synthesized by using the chemical reaction between amino groups in MOF and mercapto groups of 2,5-Dimercapto-1,3,4-thiadiazole. It has excellent reusability (see Fig. 13a) and high efficiency adsorption performance (maximum adsorption capacity is $670.5~\mathrm{mg/g}\mathrm{,}$ . The author has proved that the process of UiO-66- DMTD adsorption of mercury ions is single layer adsorption, and the adsorption method is chemical adsorption [85]. Similarly, UiO-66-NHC(S)NHMe [86] and ZIF-90-SH [90] were synthesized using this method. Among them, the removal efficiency of ZIF90-SH for trace mercury in water is within the range of $96-98\%$ And UiO-66-NHC(S)NHMe showed the most excellent mercury adsorption capacity, with the maximum adsorption capacity up to $769~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . This is the first time a sulphur-containing group has been introduced into the pores of a MOF material.
Of course, the nitrogen and sulfur co-functionalized MOF synthesized by this method can also be used for mercury ion detection. Li et al. used the free amino group on the ligand to introduce the R6G group into UiO-68 through a chemical reaction, and realized the post-synthetic-modified single crystal to single crystal, forming an efficient and sensitive mercury ion fluorescent probe (see Fig. 13b). In addition, UiO-68-R6G has good selectivity and biocompatibility, and can be used for the detection of mercury ions in organisms [91].
In order to make MOF materials have more functional groups, so as to play more roles. Yin et al. grafted two functional groups simultaneously on the MOF using a multi-component postsynthetic modification method. Thanks to the P-MCR-PSM method (Fig. 14a), the mercury adsorption capacity of the material is greatly enhanced [87]. This method also provides a simple and effective idea for post - MOF synthesis modification.
It is worth noting that, the removal of mercury ions from the material can also be achieved by introducing a special postmodification method, installing MSS (molecular building blocks). NENU-400 is unstable in mercury solution and prone to collapse. However, NENU-401 formed by introducing molecular building blocks into NENU-400 is quite stable. It can be stabilized in air for at least a week, and even in several organic solvents. It also has a good mercury removal effect, with a mercury adsorption capacity of up to $593.57~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Fig. 14b shows the schematic diagram of NENU-401 for mercury ion adsorption. Surprisingly, it is so efficient that it can remove $99.7\%$ of mercury ions in a matter of minutes [92]. This method not only expands the methods for post - modification, but also provides some ideas for the synthesis of stable materials.
## 2.4. The removal of mercury ions based on MOF composites
MOF composites are increasingly being explored and applied to mercury ion adsorption detection. The MOF composite can give itself more functions through the composite form, which broadens the application range of MOF materials. MOF composites have similar ways and approaches with functionalized MOF materials, but they have more potential applications than functionalized MOF.
Most MOF composites used to remove mercury ions are obtained by combining MOF with metal oxides or sulfides. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{TMU}-32$ had ultra-high ability to adsorb heavy metal ions. TMU-32 encapsulates the negatively charged $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles to adjust the surface charge of the composite. The negative charge on the surface of the composite forms an electrostatic effect with heavy metal cations, which is conducive to the enrichment of heavy metal ions and is then bonded to urea for removal (see Fig. 15). The mercury adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{TMU}-32$ is $905~\mathrm{mg/g}$ [93]. Although $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ TMU-32 has ultra-high mercury ion adsorption capacity, it does not have high specificity for mercury ion adsorption (it can also adsorb lead ion ultra-high), which needs to be optimized. The introduction of sulfur-containing substances into the complex can improve selectivity. Huang et al. synthesized a case of MOF composite with good selectivity for mercury ions. Bi-I-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}}$ $0_{4}@{\mathsf{S i O}_{2}@{\mathsf{H K U S T}}}-1$ (as shown in Fig. 16a) has good selective adsorption of mercury ions and fast adsorption kinetics, which can adsorb $99\%$ of mercury ions within $10\mathrm{min}$ [94]. In this article, a green and simple self-template synthesis method is reported for the first time, and it is also the first case to prepare magnetic MOF composite with core–shell structure by self-template synthesis method (see Fig. 16b). Zr-MOF (MFC-S) also has excellent selectivity for mercury ions. The maximum mercury adsorption capacity of MFC-S was
$282\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}.$ . After 5 cycles, there was almost no significant change in the adsorption capacity, indicating that MFC-S has a good recycling capacity and is very suitable for water treatment [95]. In addition, $\mathrm{SH@SiO_{2}/C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ can rapidly extract mercury ions not only from water samples, but also from fish and sediments. The MOF adsorbent has a mercury adsorption capacity of $210\mathrm{mg/g}$ and can be reused 15 times [96].
It is worth mentioning that all the MOF composites mentioned above are magnetic MOF composites. Magnetic MOF material is considered as an ideal choice for high performance adsorbent because of its excellent properties such as high specific surface area and magnetic properties. The rapid magnetic response of magnetic materials is conducive to the rapid and efficient adsorption of heavy metal ions, and also avoids the tedious separation after adsorption [95].
Similarly, the complex $\mathrm{'}\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ formed by MOF and sulfide also can remove mercury ions (as shown in Fig. 17). The large cavity of MIL-101 facilitates the rapid diffusion of mercury ions, while indium sulfide provides a large number of adsorption sites. Thanks to the synergy effect, the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ composite has good adsorption capacity $(518.2~\mathrm{mg/g}$ mercury adsorption) and ultra-fast adsorption kinetics $99.95\%$ mercury ions can be removed in one minute) [97]. Moreover, $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ can be prepared on a large scale without affecting the adsorption performance, which is beneficial to practical applications. Polysulfides can also be combined with MOF to form a composite that can remove mercury ions. Even the distribution coefficient of S4- MOFs to mercury ions can be as high as 7 orders of magnitude [98]. However, it should be noted that polysulfides are sensitive to oxygen in the air, which increases the difficulty of material synthesis. In order to avoid air sensitivity of ammonia and sulphide substances, Samanta et al. cleverly took advantage of reactions between acetylene groups and mercury ions to form a fluorescent probe (UiO-66@butyne). Althrough the irreversible reaction that forms mercury oxide improves sensitivity (detection limit $10.9~\mathrm{{nM}}\cdot$ ) and accuracy [99], it limits material recycling.
The introduction of precious metals was also very effective. Of course, MOF and precious metal compound also has a good effect of removing mercury. (Pt NP) $\varpi\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}}$ can be sensitive detection and adsorb mercury ions efficiently. It has peroxidelike activity and can oxidize TMB in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . However, mercury ions can affect the oxidation of TMB by substitution with Pt nanoparticles (as shown in Fig. 18), resulting in varying degrees of weakening in the UV spectrum. Therefore, the concentration of mercury ions can be detected and the detection limit is about $0.35{\mathrm{~nM}}$ , which is far lower than the concentration of mercury ions stipulated in drinking water. The complex also has an efficient mercury ion adsorption capacity, removing more than $99\%$ of the mercury ions, even at very high initial concentrations [100]. Also, Xu et al. realized the simultaneous detection and adsorption of mercury ions by encapsulating activated carbon dots in MOF. The MOF composite has excellent selectivity and sensitivity, and the detection limit is $13\ \mathrm{ppb}$ . In addition, chelation between mercury ions and CDs (carbon dots) also facilitates the adsorption and removal of mercury ions by this material, with the removal efficiency of above $90\%$ [101].
## 2.5. The removal of mercury ions based on MOFs-derived functional inorganic materials
MOFs derived materials have attracted more and more attention due to their high stability and high activity. It not only maintains the large specific surface area and high porosity of MOFs, but also effectively avoids the disadvantage of unstable MOF materials under severe conditions. MOFs derived materials are usually generated by high-temperature pyrolysis and carbonization of MOFs. MOFs-derived functional inorganic materials have the advantages of high surface area, various possible pore sizes and the ability to expand inorganic nanomaterials with new properties. Although MOFs are expected to be precursors for the synthesis of new materials, the expensive carbonation reaction hinders their batch preparation and large-scale application. Therefore, a wet treatment method was proposed to obtain MOFs derived inorganic materials under mild conditions. The functionalized inorganic material 68 (W) was obtained by the wet treatment of ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ instead of ligands in MIL-68 (as shown in Fig. 19a). The nanopores of 68 (W) facilitate the rapid diffusion of mercury ions, and the uniformly distributed high content of sulfur quickly chelate mercury ions. Thanks to the synergy between porosity and high sulfur content, $2.2{\mathrm{~g~}}$ of mercury ions can be absorbed per gram of adsorbent. Even the distribution coefficient of mercury ions is as high as $6.8\times10^{6}\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{g}[\$ 102]. The mercury removal capacity of this material is amazing. It is worth mentioning that thiol-functionalized inorganic materials were obtained through this new and simple wet treatment method, which is impossible to achieve by carbonization, and also provides ideas for future industrial applications. ZrSulf, as a functional inorganic material, also has good mercury ion adsorption capacity and good selectivity[103]. Of course, MOFs derivatives have also been extended to bifunctional materials that can adsorb and detect mercury ions. CuS obtained by wet treatment of HKUST-1, which effectively removed mercury ions and expanded the application of mercury ion colorimetric detection. Similar to $(\mathsf{P t}\ \mathsf{N P})@\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}}$ , CuS can also oxidize TMB in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . However, the addition of mercury ions can inhibit the oxidation of TMB. Based on this, the author constructed a sensor with high sensitivity and high selectivity for mercury ion detection (see Fig. 19b). In addition, CuS has a significant adsorption capacity of $2105\ \mathrm{mg/g}$ on mercury ions [104]. However, due to the strong interaction between mercury ion and sulfur ion, the material cannot be recycled, which is still to be improved in the future.
## 3. The removal of mercury ions based on MOF membranes
The characteristics of MOF, such as large specific surface area, high porosity and easy modification, are conducive to the rapid diffusion of mercury ions to the adsorption site, accelerating the kinetic process of adsorption, and maintaining a high saturation adsorption capacity. However, MOF powder materials also face problems of low recycling efficiency and the possibility of secondary pollution. And the direct use of MOFs to remove heavy metals is limited to laboratory-scale experiments. Therefore, the modification design based on powder materials has also been paid more and more attention. Among them, MOF membranes not only have the structural characteristics of MOF powder materials, but also have the high performance of membrane materials. MOF membranes are easy to recycle, and effectively avoid secondary pollution. Therefore, some scholars tried to combine MOF materials with polymer to prepare MOF membranes for exploring the adsorption capacity of heavy metal ions. In view of the diversity of membrane types, in this part we will expand on the mercury removal capabilities of the two types of membranes: Nanofiber MOF membranes, and polymer-supported MOF membranes.
## 3.1. Nanofiber MOF membrane
Nanofiber MOF membranes (NMOM) are generally obtained by electrospinning. Electrospinning [105] is a technique for spinning polymer solutions under the action of high voltage static electricity (see Fig. 20). Dozens of polymers, such as polyacrylonitrile, polyethylene glycol, polyvinylidene fluoride, can be used for electrostatic spinning. The obtained fibers are nanoscale with large porosity and high specific surface area. Because of the advantages of easy operation and controllable size, it is widely used in water treatment, sensing, catalysis, and drug loading.
In view of the advantages of MOF powder material in heavy metal ion removal, as well as the large porosity and high specific surface area of electrostatic spinning fiber. Therefore, an attempt was made to introduce MOF into polymer solution to form nanofiber MOF membrane. And there is good compatibility between MOF and polymer. This makes it more feasible to remove heavy metal ions.
Efome et al. prepared the nanofiber MOF membrane by cospinning with MOF-808 and PAN. The film consists of two layers, the upper layer is PAN/MOF-808 fiber film, which is used to adsorb heavy metal ions [106,107]. The lower layer is pure PVDF fiber membrane, which is used to control the flux of water, and the two synergically achieve the removal of heavy metal ions (see Fig. 21). Different with MOF nanofiltration membranes, which can increase the efficiency of nanofiltration with increased pressure. Increasing the pressure will reduce the porosity of the nanofiber MOF membranes. Therefore, the membrane filtration process of nanofiber MOF membranes is carried out at a pressure of 0.01 MPa. Although this method is simple to operate, it has low selectivity for heavy metal ions and low removal efficiency. Among them, the removal efficiency of mercury ions is only $34\%$ . Therefore, the authors propose an unconventional, green and simple activation method, ’water activation’, which can increase the pore volume of MOF-808, thus increasing the number of adsorption sites [108]. This gives it considerable advantages in sewage treatment. In addition, the authors introduced water-stable MOF-808 and the amorphous structure of MOF-F300 into the nanofiber MOF membrane. The stability of the composite MOF nanofiber membrane is better than that of the single MOF membrane. The large pore size of the MOF material makes it easier for mercury ions to diffuse in the pores, and the mercury ions are electrostatically attracted to the richer COO- groups in the pore size, allowing mercury ions to be stored in the MOF. Moreover, it has good repeatability. After four cycles, the performance remains almost unchanged. Importantly, the high concentration of mercury ions can be removed by stacking the number of membranes [109]. Similarly, PAN/chitosan/UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ nanofiber MOF membrane also be used to remove heavy metal ions [110].
NMOM has very small requirements on the amount of polymer used, and the post-treatment process of the membrane is also very small and simple. The large surface area, high porosity, high flux, high efficiency and low consumption, and reusable performance make it have great potential in membrane separation/adsorption treatment of water. However, it also faces the poor continuity of one-dimensional nanofibers and the inherent limitations of nanocharacteristics that are difficult to maintain, which limits its application in more aspects. Zhang et al. proposed a new type of processing technology - electrostatic spray screen, and the resulting two-dimensional nanomaterials can make up for the defects of one-dimensional materials [111]. The introduction of MOF materials into two-dimensional nanomaterials to form membranes to remove heavy metals should have great development space in the future.
## 3.2. Polymer supported MOF membranes
Polymer supported membranes have attracted much attention because of their advantages of both polymer membranes and MOF materials. Moreover, due to the compatibility between the ligand of MOF and the polymer, it is easier to prepare the polymer supporting MOF membrane. Moreover, it can avoid the agglomeration of MOF particles skillfully and improve the performance of the membrane. At present, MOF is mainly supported on polymer membranes by in-situ synthesis. Because in situ synthesis is simple, and can maintain the structure and characteristics of MOF. In order to avoid the problem of weak force and insufficient contact between MOF and substrate, which leads to the shedding of MOF or uneven density of MOF, the substrate is usually modified in advance. Yang et al. prepared a polymer-supported ZIF membrane by in-situ synthesis of ZIF material on a flexible substrate modified with sodium carboxymethyl cellulose (see Fig. 22). The carboxymethylation modification of the substrate causes the hydroxyl groups on the substrate to form hydrogen bonds with the metal ions, thereby obtaining uniform nucleation sites on the substrate and enhancing the adhesion between the MOF and the substrate. Nitrogen and oxygen in ZIF can coordinate with heavy metal ions, so this polymer-supported membrane can be well used in water purification [112]. The substrate can also be modified with PEI [113,114], so that the MOF material can grow uniformly on the substrate without falling off.
Increasing the loading of MOF on the substrate is the key to prepare polymer- supported membrane, so it is necessary to develop suitable substrate materials. Nanofiber membranes substrate have become the first choice due to their large specific surface area and uniform macropores. The ESF@ZIF composite membrane was prepared by depositing MOF material on the fiber ESF membrane. The bionic mineralization strategy formed continuous nucleation and binding sites on the substrate, which increased the loading rate and density. Most importantly, the ESF@ZIF composite membrane retains the high porosity of the MOF material. This strategy also provides a new idea for increasing the MOF load. The hydrogen groups and amino groups formed on the surface of ZIF serve as adsorption sites for heavy metal ions, and the removal efficiency is nearly $100\%$ [115]. The use of simple and efficient methods to prepare high-load MOF filter membranes has great application potential in wastewater treatment.
The choice of substrate is also diverse. Liu et al. chose cheaper filter paper as polymer substrate and synthesized ZIF-8 filter membrane in situ. After vulcanization treatment, ZnS-ZIF-8 filter membrane is formed, as shown in the Fig. 23. Based on the interaction of ion exchange mechanism and chelation mechanism, the ZnS-ZIF-8 filter membrane realized the rapid removal of mercury ions. More than $99\%$ mercury ions can be removed within two minutes, with a maximum adsorption capacity of $925.9~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . In addition, it also has extremely high selectivity and recyclability [116]. The ZnSZIF-8 filter membrane has realized the simple, rapid and efficient removal of mercury ions and is a potential sewage treatment material. The lower economic cost makes it possible to be widely used in the treatment of a large number of industrial sewage.
The polymer substrate used in the polymer supported membrane is economical and retains the characteristics of the MOF material. Through simple in-situ synthesis, the porous material is combined with an inexpensive substrate to prepare a polymer supported membrane that can quickly and efficiently remove heavy metal ions in water. Although the unreliable and insufficient contact between the MOF material and the substrate restricts the widespread application of polymer supported membranes. However, these problems can be avoided by simply modifying the substrate. In general, the advantages of easy operation, cost saving, and good performance make it have great application prospects in sewage treatment, and also provide ideas for the preparation of new MOF membranes.
## 4. Adsorption mechanism of mercury ions on MOF materials
MOF materials and MOF membrane have been well used in removing mercury ions. In order to understand the adsorption process of mercury ions by MOF more clearly, the adsorption kinetics of mercury ions has been deeply studied.
Adsorption kinetics can reflect the adsorption rate and reaction pathway of adsorbents. Moreover, it is a key parameter to evaluate adsorption mechanism and practical application. To date, the kinetic models such as the pseudo-first-order dynamics model, the pseudo-second-order dynamics model, the W-M dynamics model, and the Bangham channel diffusion model have been proposed. In order to understand the adsorption process more clearly, the kinetic equation is usually used to fit the adsorption data. Luo et al. fitted the adsorption data of mercury ions by MOF-74 [51], and found that the experimental data were more consistent with the pesudo-first-order kinetic equation. The first–second-order kinetic equation is:
$$
\ln({\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}-{\bf q}_{\mathrm{t}})=\ln{\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}-{\bf k}_{1}{\bf t}
$$
$\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ represents the adsorption capacity when the adsorption equilibrium is reached, that is, the saturated adsorption capacity, qt represents the amount of adsorption at time t, $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ is the rate constant, t is time.
However, the calculation of saturated adsorption is more consistent with the pesudo-second-order kinetic equation.(The pseudo-second-order kinetic equation is:
$$
\mathrm{t}/\up q_{\mathrm{t}}=1/(\updownarrow_{2}\up q_{\mathrm{e}2})=\up t/\up q_{\mathrm{e}}
$$
, $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ is the rate constant) Therefore, it can be explained that the adsorption process of MOF-74 for mercury ions conforms to the pesudo-first-order and pesudo-second-order kinetic equations, and both physical adsorption and chemical adsorption are involved in the adsorption process of mercury ions. By fitting the kinetic data of Zr-MSA MOF on mercury ion adsorption, Gu et al. found that the adsorption process was completely consistent with the pseudosecond-order kinetic adsorption model, and the correlation coefficient $\mathsf{R}^{2}=1$ [52]. By fitting the actual adsorption data, the closer the correlation coefficient is to 1, the more the adsorption kinetics conforms to the adsorption process of the pseudo-second-order kinetic model. Because the correlation coefficient of Zr-MSA adsorption on mercury ions is equal to 1, it can be deduced that chemical adsorption is the main rate-determining step in the adsorption process [52].W-M kinetics is often used to analyze the control steps in the reaction. The adsorption process of the material is divided into two adsorption processes: the surface adsorption of the adsorbent and the slow diffusion of the pores. In the adsorption process, the Bangham channel diffusion model is used to describe the channel diffusion mechanism.
In view of the different adsorption mechanisms guided by different kinetic models, the kinetic models of different types of MOF for the adsorption of mercury ions are listed in the Table 4.
The discussion of the specific adsorption process and sites is also beneficial to the further understanding of the adsorption mechanism. The adsorption of mercury ions by MOF materials generally includes physical adsorption, chemical adsorption and ion exchange adsorption. In Table 5, Mn-MOF [50] remove mercury ions through physical adsorption, because the pure MOF without any modification has no chemical bond interaction with mercury ions. However, for sulfurized MOF [52,54,57–61,64,65], MOF modified with the sulfur-containing functional groups can achieve chemisorption of mercury ions through sulfur groups. Similarly, based on the coordination interaction between nitrogen and sulfur groups and mercury ions, nitrogen and sulfur coligand-based MOF [85–87,92] also realize the chemisorption of mercury ions. In addition, Maji et al. prepared an anionic MOF by a post-synthetic metallization method, and achieved the adsorption of mercury ions through an ion exchange process [118].
At the same time, the adsorption site is also an important factor, and the design of functional MOF generally needs to take into account the influence of the final adsorption site. For example, if the pore size of the MOF is small, it is not appropriate to introduce adsorption sites into the pores of the MOF. Therefore, the adsorption site is often related to designable frame structure of MOF. Xu et al. [59] introduced sulfur side chains into MOF ligands, and then it existed in MOF channels. Mercury ions enter the MOF channel and coordinate with the sulfur side chain. The adsorption site is in the channel of MOF. For pure MOF without any modification, the site of adsorption of mercury ions is the pore of the MOF. However, for sulfurized MOF, ammoniated MOF and nitrogen and sulfur coligand-based MOF, removal of mercury ions mainly depends on the functional groups introduced into MOF. Therefore, the position of the functional group is the adsorption site of mercury ions. In general, for most sulfurized or ammoniated MOFs, the introduced functional groups are generally present in the pores of the MOF. Therefore, the adsorption position is in the pores of the MOF. For example, Xu et al. [54] introduced the modified sulfhydryl group on the ligand into the pores of Zr-DMBD, and the adsorption site of mercury ions occurred in the MOF pores. However, there are also adsorption locations on the channel walls of MOF. Wang et al adopted the strategy of post-synthetic modification to fix thioglycolic acid on the channel walls of MiL-68 [64]. The mercury ions entering MOF are captured by the sulfhydryl group in thioglycolic acid. In addition, Liu et al. functionally modified $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ with 2,5-dimercapto-1,3,4-thiadiazole, and it was attached to the surface of the MOF. The adsorption sites of mercury ions is the surface of UiO-66-DMTD [85].
Finally, the effect of the features of MOF material on the adsorption of mercury ions was discussed. The design of the MOF frame structure, large specific surface area, controllable pore size and other characteristics of MOF materials promote the more efficient adsorption of mercury ions. However, what plays an important role is the design of the frame structure. Because the design of the framework can introduce specific functional groups into the MOF material, and it is the decisive factor for the adsorption and removal of mercury ions. Xu et al. introduced mercapto functional group into MOF ligand and realized the efficient removal of mercury ions by utilizing the strong coordination between mercapto group and mercury ions, with the removal efficiency exceeding $99.9\%$ [54]. In addition, Luo et al. introduced nitrogen-containing functional groups into MOF through the mixed-ligand strategy, and achieved efficient removal of mercury ions by utilizing the coordination interaction between mercury ions and nitrogencontaining functional groups [72]. Salam et al. prepared Mn-MOF by microwave method, and used the pore structure of MOF to adsorb mercury ions [50]. However, the removal efficiency of mercury ions was only $64\%$ . Compared with the above two cases of MOF, by designing the frame structure of MOF, the chemical capture of mercury ions was realized to avoid the escape of mercury ions, and the removal efficiency was improved. Therefore, reasonable design and regulation of MOF frame structure can improve the performance of MOF.
In addition, for some MOFs with large specific surface area and high porosity, adsorption sites are abundant. This is conducive to the adsorption of more mercury ions, but also can realize the efficient removal of mercury ions. Similarly, the larger the pore size of an MOF, the greater the amount of mercury ions it can hold. For example, Hong et al. chose MIL-101 with large cavity to adsorb mercury ions [97].
## 5. Stability of MOF materials
Although MOF materials have made great contributions to the removal of mercury ions. However, it is not difficult to find that most of the MOF materials used in mercury ion removal in water are UiO, MIL and ZIF. As the classic water-stabilized MOFs materials, they are mainly based on the theory of soft and hard acid-base to improve the stability of MOF [119]. In practical applications, other types of MOF materials often cannot be used for mercury ion removal because they do not have good water stability. In order to solve this problem, broaden the application of more types of MOF in the field of mercury ion removal. The following strategies have been proposed:
## (1) Ligand modification
Su et al. [120] introduced pyridine nitrogen and hydrophobic group (-F) into the organic ligand to increase the rigidity and hydrophobicity of the ligand, and effectively improved the water stability of TB-MOF-A ([Tb(TFTBA)1.5(phen) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right).$ N), which could maintain stability for up to 21 days in aqueous solution. The same strategy has been applied to the dual-luminescent lanthanide MOF [121], where Eu0.07Gd0.03-MOF can even be preserved in water for 28 days without structural damage and without affecting the luminescent properties of the material. The rigidity of ligands can enhance the stability of the framework, and the introduction of hydrophobic groups can effectively prevent water from attacking the metal–oxygen coordination bonds. And by introducing appropriate functional groups, while maintaining the stability of MOFs materials, it can also broaden the applications of MOFs materials.
(2) Metal protection
Metal protection strategy is also an effective method to improve the water stability of MOF. Jiao et al. [122] introduced a small amount of Ni into Mg-MOF-74 to compete for ligand coordination. As a metal ion that is not easy to hydrolyze, nickel ion may help MOF-74 to obtain partial kinetic water stability. The results show that the introduction of Ni can effectively improve the stability of Mg-MOF-74. On this basis, Sholl et al. [123] analyzed the possible reasons through theoretical calculation. After the introduction of Ni, the metal–oxygen bond in the metal oxide skeleton of MOF material changes obviously. The stability of Mg-Ni-MOF-74 is due to the long distortion of M-O bond.
## (3) Post-synthetic modification
Post-synthetic modification is also a powerful method to improve the stability of materials. Fan et al. [124] used the uncoordinated carboxylic acid group on the MOF to modify and connect to the hydrophobic alkyl chain. At the same time, through the crystal size and morphology control, a superhydrophobic MOF-based aerogel was prepared for efficient oil–water separation. The material still maintains high stability after 50 cycles of experiments. The stability can also be improved by modifying the pores of the MOF material. Rosi et al. [125] introduced a series of aliphatic monocarboxylic acids into the pores of the MOF through metal source. The aliphatic monocarboxylic acid adjusts the pores and hydrophobicity of the material, and the longer the length of the fatty chain, the higher the stability of the material. Among them, bio-MOF-14 can be stored stably in water for one month. Zhang et al. used the shielding effect to protect the stability of the material. A onestep method is used to directly graft cholesterol on the surface of the MOF [126]. The cholesterol acts as a water transport protective layer to improve the water stability of the material. Even if the material is treated with water for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the porosity and crystallinity are still intact. In addition, PVP and P123 can also be coated on the surface of the MOF to enhance the hydrophobicity of the MOF surface [127]. Importantly, the same method used to form a water transport protective layer on the surface of MOF can also be applied to MOF membrane materials. Kim et al. used the amino group free on the oleamine to coordinate with the metal site on the surface of MOF membrane, thus forming a watercarrying fat layer on the surface of MOF membrane to protect the MOF material from water attack [128].
molecules near the metal center, thereby improving the moisture resistance of the MOF material. Park et al. heat-treated the surface of IRMOF-1 to form a layer of inorganic carbon coating on the surface of the MOF, which effectively protects the stability of the MOF frame [131]. This more general and environmentally friendly strategy will be expected to be used for commercial preparation of water-stable MOF materials. In addition, heat treatment of the hydrolyzed MOF can obtain a MOF-derived inorganic material with pore regeneration. The performance loss caused by hydrolysis can be dealt with by simple heat treatment, which is different from the previous strategy to protect the structure of MOF from being destroyed. The proposal of this method broadens the practicability of MOF from another perspective.
## 6. Recycling of MOF materials
The cyclic regeneration capacity of the adsorbent is an important factor in judging the performance of the adsorbent. The disposable adsorbent is not economical and practical, and a lot of time is spent on the preparation of the adsorbent. Therefore, adsorbents with good recycling ability are favored by people.
Eluent is usually used to elute the mercury ions adsorbed on MOF material, and then MOF material can be regenerated. At present, there are two types of eluents. One is sulfur-containing substances. It has a stronger coordination effect with mercury ions, and the mercury ions are deprived from the MOF material through coordination. Tadjarodi et al. [132] used thiourea to regenerate the MOF, and the regenerated MOF can repeatedly use 12 times. In addition, $\mathsf{S H@S i O_{2}C u_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ regenerated with thiourea can be recycled 15 times [96]. In addition to thiourea, ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf S}$ [100,116], 2- mercaptoethanol [60], thioglycol [92], EDTA-2Na [117], KSCN [82] and so on are also excellent eluents of mercury ions. In particular, the regeneration of thioether MOF material can be achieved by stirring in eluent solution at room temperature. However, mercaptan MOF can be regenerated only under the auxiliary conditions of strong acid, strong base or certain temperature because of its stronger coordination with mercury ions [60]. Another type of eluent is strong acid (hydrochloric acid [52], nitric acid [71]). Under acidic conditions, a large number of hydrogen ions or hydronium ions compete for coordination with mercury ions, and the desorption of mercury ions is achieved by occupying the coordination sites of mercury ions. The influence of the concentration and the acidity and basicity of the solution should be taken into account, otherwise, it cause the permanent and irreversible damage to the MOF material. The good regeneration rate and excellent cycle performance of MOF materials make them well used in the removal of mercury ions.
## 7. Conclusion and outlook
## (4) others
The stability of MOF materials can also be improved by forming composite materials. HKUST-1 can grow spherically in super-large mesoporous FDU-12 silica substrate [129]. The hydrophobicity of the silica substrate prevents the entry and diffusion of water and protects the frame of the MOF material. Even if it is immersed in $50\ \mathrm{^\circC}$ water for one day, the structure of the material will not be destroyed.
Zhang et al. realized the first method of improving stability through ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S$ gas treatment [130]. The hydrogen sulfide gas is coordinated with the metal sites, so that it exists in the MOF framework, and then absorbs water molecules that enter the MOF pores and can destroy the MOF structure. ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}S$ consumes water
Simple and efficient removal of mercury ions from the source of drinking water is essential. As a popular material for research in recent years, MOF materials have become the preferred choice for people because of their high porosity, large specific surface area, and ease of modification. This review revolves around MOF materials and MOF membranes to explore their applications in mercury ion removal. For MOF powder material, proper modification can optimize the properties of the material. For example, removal efficiency and time can be improved by functionalizing or forming MOF composites. However, to achieve industrial applications, MOF powder materials are far from enough. Thus, people explored more forms of MOF, for example, MOF membrane. Because of the characteristics of MOF and high performance of membrane, MOF membrane has great application potential in sewage treatment. Moreover, the purpose of removing a large amount of heavy metal ions in water can be achieved by stacking the number of membranes. In general, this review aims to provide more researchers with a more intuitive and clear understanding of the MOF and MOF membrane on heavy metal ion removal.
At present, there is relatively little research on the application of MOF membranes in the removal of heavy metal ions. Therefore, for MOF powder materials and MOF membranes materials, we put forward several aspects that need to be paid attention to in the future:
## (1) Improve the water stability of the material
Whether MOF powder or MOF membranes, the premise for sewage treatment is that the material must be stable in water. The UiO, ZIF, and MIL series of MOFs materials have good water stability, so they are more explored in sewage treatment. The stability of these three types of MOF materials is due to the fact that the high-valence metals form a strong coordination bond with the ligands. In addition, the stability of the material can be improved by introducing hydrophobic functional groups [120,133,134] or adopting hydrophobic embedding strategy [135–137]. The water stability of MOFs is influenced by many factors, such as ligand rigidity, coordination number and so on [138]. These influencing factors can be taken into consideration to prepare super-waterstable MOFs materials, so as to improve the long-term cycling performance of materials. As a kind of covalent organic frame material, COF has been used more and more in water treatment [139– 144] because of its good water stability. It is hoped that in the future, more water-stable MOFs and COF materials can be explored and used in water treatment efficiently.
## (2) Broaden the types of applicable powder materials
Metal organic frame materials are a large branch of porous frame materials. It is not enough that only a few kinds of MOF materials can be used in water treatment. As a fascinating material, MOF has many places worth exploring and digging. The diversity of the coordination environment, coordination methods, metals, and the variety and the designability of ligands and so on give the MOF materials colorful configurations. The development of more newer types of MOF materials has been the focus of research by scientists in this field. In the future, more attention should be paid to simple, green and diverse synthesis methods to design colorful MOF material types.
## (3) Diversity of MOF membrane
At present, MOF membrane preparation methods have been various, such as in-situ method, crystal seed method, interfacial polymerization method and so on [145–147]. However, MOF membranes prepared by these methods are usually used for gas adsorption and selective separation. The preparation technology of MOF membrane for water treatment is not very mature. Although nanofiber MOF membranes and supported MOF membranes have gradually come into people’s sight, the research in this field is not indepth and detailed enough. Therefore, the preparation of MOF membranes for water treatment should be focused. Explore more diverse membrane forming methods and learn mature membrane forming technology, so that more types of MOF powder materials can be prepared into membranes and applied to water treatment.
## (4) Focus on highly sensitive detection of mercury ions
The World Health Organization limits the content of mercury ions in drinking water to $\mathbf{\Psi}<\mathbf{\Psi}1$ ppb. This low emission standard not only increases the difficulty of removal, but it is also a challenge in the detection of mercury ions. At present, people usually use high-end instruments such as ICP, ICP-MS, ICP-OES and AFS to detect the content of heavy metal ions. Expensive equipment and precise operation limit its wide application. How to detect mercury ions with low cost, easy operation and high sensitivity is a difficult problem concerned by people. Halder et al. proposed the visual detection of mercury ions [83], which is a breakthrough in this field. However, its detection limit and sensitivity did not reach our expectation. Therefore, more efforts will be needed in the future. In addition, we should also open up our thinking and design more dual-function materials. To achieve the purpose of simultaneous detection and removal of heavy metal ions. Such dual-functional materials have huge development potential in the field of water treatment.
Exploring water-stable porous frame materials and porous frame membranes is the current opportunity and challenge for water treatment. The constraints of the types of MOF powder materials and the immaturity of MOFs membrane technology are destined to have a long way to go. However, it is expected that MOF materials will play a huge role in the field of water treatment. MOF membranes, in particular, will shine.
